From 1c8671d873aa2fb415e163cd02036f0d1a5df579 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Simon Chrzanowski <simonch@gmx.de>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 10:32:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fftools/ffmpeg_sched: fix decoder starvation in
 unchoke_downstream()

03dfac56 ("fftools/ffmpeg_sched: allow throttling decoder outputs") gated the
downstream traversal on the decoder's own choked_next:

    case SCH_NODE_TYPE_DEC:
        dec = &sch->dec[dst->idx];
        if (!dec->waiter.choked_next) {
            for (int i = 0; i < dec->nb_outputs; i++)
                unchoke_downstream(sch, dec->outputs[i].dst);
        }
        break;

schedule_update_locked() resets every choked_next at the top of the pass and
clears them one stream at a time as it walks the graph, so whether this gate
lets the traversal through depends on whether this particular decoder happened
to be cleared earlier in the same pass. When it blocks, everything downstream
of the decoder stays choked, the decoder's output backs up and it stops
draining its own overflow FIFO.

With two output streams this starves one decoder for most of the run. On a
4742 s MPEG-TS capture transcoded to video+audio, counting packets in and out
of the overflow FIFO gives:

    dec0 video  in=234477 parked=234387 served=103314 direct=90   dropped=0
    dec1 audio  in=197582 parked=195399 served=195399 direct=2183 dropped=0

Audio drains completely; video parks 234387 packets and is served 103314. The
missing 131073 are never dropped anywhere - they sit in the FIFO until
teardown, so ffmpeg exits 0, prints no warning and shows no drop= in the stats
line while writing 42% of the video frames. The count varies between runs of
the identical command because the traversal order does.

Reproduced with:

    ffmpeg -i capture.ts -map 0:v:0 -map 0:a:0 \
      -vf "bwdif=mode=send_field,scale=round(iw*sar/16)*16:trunc(ih/2)*2" \
      -c:v libx264 -preset ultrafast -g 50 -c:a aac -f null -

Verified after removing the gate: 237062 frames on five consecutive runs, where
the same command previously produced 96314 to 110064. An unaffected capture is
unchanged at 207740, and video-only, which never regressed, is unchanged.
Reverting 03dfac56 wholesale yields the same 237062, so this restores the
pre-commit behaviour while keeping the decoder throttling the commit adds.
---
 fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c | 10 ++++++----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c b/fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c
index 8ec3bdaf80..5979eaa104 100644
--- a/fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c
+++ b/fftools/ffmpeg_sched.c
@@ -1338,10 +1338,12 @@ static void unchoke_downstream(Scheduler *sch, SchedulerNode *dst)
     switch (dst->type) {
     case SCH_NODE_TYPE_DEC:
         dec = &sch->dec[dst->idx];
-        if (!dec->waiter.choked_next) {
-            for (int i = 0; i < dec->nb_outputs; i++)
-                unchoke_downstream(sch, dec->outputs[i].dst);
-        }
+        // Not gated on dec->waiter.choked_next: schedule_update_locked()
+        // resets those at the top of every pass and clears them as it walks
+        // the graph, so gating here makes the traversal depend on the order
+        // in which streams happen to be visited.
+        for (int i = 0; i < dec->nb_outputs; i++)
+            unchoke_downstream(sch, dec->outputs[i].dst);
         break;
     case SCH_NODE_TYPE_ENC:
         enc = &sch->enc[dst->idx];
